{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. Johnson. I'm Dr. Patel, one of your hospital doctors. I understand you’ve been feeling more tired and short of breath lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, doctor. It started about four days ago. I get winded just walking a block, and today I felt so dizzy and breathless even at rest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you used your nitroglycerin in the ER earlier?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:19:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, one tablet under the tongue. It helped after about 20 minutes. But I didn’t have chest pain—just shortness of breath and fatigue."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s important. Your EKG showed some changes, but not in the area we’d expect if your stent was blocked. We’re concerned about whether this is heart-related or something else."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:22:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had chest pressure before, but this feels different. More like exhaustion and breathlessness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Sometimes heart issues can present as fatigue or breathlessness instead of classic chest pain. We’ve started you on a heparin drip to prevent clots, just in case."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:25:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I was on Coumadin. Why stop it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good question. We paused Coumadin because of the possible heart issue. Heparin works faster and is safer during acute evaluation. We’ll restart Coumadin soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:28:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And my INR was okay before coming in?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2189-12-26 20:29:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it was therapeutic when you arrived. We’ll monitor it closely. We also ordered a CT scan to check for blood clots in your lungs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2189-12-27 09:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your CT scan from last night showed no pulmonary embolism. In fact, your previous clots have resolved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2189-12-27 09:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good news. So why am I still so tired?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2189-12-27 09:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re still evaluating. The scan did show some mild changes at the lung bases, but they’re likely not the cause. We’re thinking this might be related to your heart."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2189-12-27 09:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My heart? But I had a stent placed recently. Could it be blocked again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2189-12-27 09:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a possibility we need to rule out. We’re planning a cardiac catheterization tomorrow to check your stents and arteries."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2189-12-27 09:24:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will it be through the wrist again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2189-12-27 09:25:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, same as last time—through the right radial artery. It’s less invasive and heals faster."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2189-12-27 09:27:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I trust the team. Just want to feel better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, Mr. Johnson. Just back from your cardiac cath. Good news—your OM1 stent is open, and there are no new blockages."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So nothing’s blocked? Then why do I feel this way?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your symptoms are likely due to chronic stable angina. Even with stents, the underlying disease can cause fatigue and breathlessness with activity."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not an emergency, but it’s still heart-related?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:37:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. It’s not an acute blockage, but your heart isn’t getting enough oxygen during exertion, which causes these symptoms."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What will we do about it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:41:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re starting you on a new medication called isosorbide mononitrate—brand name Imdur. It’s a long-acting nitroglycerin that helps prevent symptoms."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:42:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s like my SL nitro, but works all day?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, exactly. You’ll take one pill each morning. You still keep the sublingual nitro for sudden symptoms."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:45:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And my Coumadin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re restarting your warfarin today at your home dose. Since it takes a few days to work, you’ll also get a daily Lovenox injection until your INR is back in range."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:48:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "How will I know when to stop the Lovenox?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "A visiting nurse will check your INR on Thursday. Once it’s between 2.0 and 3.0 for two days, you’ll stop Lovenox 48 hours later."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:51:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. And my other meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "All your usual medications continue—Plavix, aspirin, atorvastatin, metoprolol, valsartan, and your insulin. We’re keeping everything consistent."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2189-12-28 13:54:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I’ve been checking my blood sugar—it’s been stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2189-12-29 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning, Mr. Johnson. You’re looking well. We’re planning to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2189-12-29 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. So my heart is okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2189-12-29 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your heart function is stable. The cath showed no new issues. Your symptoms are from chronic angina, which we’re now treating with Imdur."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2189-12-29 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And I’ll follow up with my cardiologist?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2189-12-29 08:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your PCP will coordinate follow-up. Make sure to call if you have chest pain, worsening shortness of breath, or any bleeding from the Lovenox."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2189-12-29 08:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you, doctor. I feel much better already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2189-12-29 08:10:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll have the nurse go over your discharge instructions and medications before you leave."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2189-12-29 10:15:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Doctor, one more thing—should I avoid any activities?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2189-12-29 10:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can resume normal activities, but listen to your body. If you get short of breath or tired, rest. Avoid heavy lifting for a few days due to the Lovenox."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2189-12-29 10:18:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Understood. And the bruising at my wrist from the cath?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2189-12-29 10:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Mild bruising is normal. It should fade in a few days. Let us know if it swells or becomes painful."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2189-12-29 10:21:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will do. Thanks again for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2189-12-29 10:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Of course. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2189-12-26 20:08:00",
    "admission_end": "2189-12-29 14:35:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with progressive fatigue and shortness of breath over four days, initially evaluated for possible stent restenosis or pulmonary embolism. He had no chest pain but reported dyspnea at rest improved with sublingual nitroglycerin. CT angiography showed resolution of prior pulmonary emboli with no new clots, and cardiac catheterization revealed no in-stent restenosis or new coronary lesions. His symptoms were attributed to chronic stable angina, and he was started on isosorbide mononitrate for symptom control. Anticoagulation was transitioned from heparin back to warfarin with bridging Lovenox. The patient remained stable, euvolemic, and without arrhythmias. He was discharged home with updated medications and follow-up plans.",
    "problems": [
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "Chronic diastolic heart failure",
      "Other and unspecified angina pectoris",
      "Congestive heart failure, unspecified",
      "Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty status",
      "Personal history of venous thrombosis and embolism",
      "Long-term (current) use of anticoagulants",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Other and unspecified hyperlipidemia",
      "Long-term (current) use of insulin",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism"
    ]
  }
}
